#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two pills is drawn from a population. The population has pills of two different active ingredient concentrations, $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $\\textrm{15}\\,\\textrm{mg/ml},$ distributed in the ratio $1:2.$ Calculate $\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pills in the population have concentrations of $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+2 = 3$ pills with only the following concentrations:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(30+2\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{3} \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{mg/ml},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({30}^{2}+2\\cdot {15}^{2})-{20}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(900+450)-400 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1\\,350-400 \\\\  & =450-400 \\\\  & =50\\,(\\text{mg/ml})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample size $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] =\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} = \\dfrac{50}{2} = 25\\,(\\textrm{mg/ml})^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083" :question/problem "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{200}$ is a sample of size $n=200$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu = 23$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 4,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overline{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is $\\sigma^2 = 4$ and the sample size is $n=200,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{4}{200} \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{0.02} \\approx 0.14 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ of $n$ coconuts is drawn from a population. The population has coconuts of two different weights, $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb},$ distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Determine the smallest sample size required to give a standard error of less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coconuts in the population have weights $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+3 = 5$ coconuts with only the following weights:\n\n$$\n1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 1.5+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =1.8\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot {1.5}^{2}+3\\cdot {2}^{2})-{1.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(16.5)-3.24 \\\\  & =3.3-3.24 \\\\  & =0.06\\,{\\text{lb}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma= \\sqrt{0.06},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.06}{ n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{{0.02}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{0.0004}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{150} \\\\ n & >150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=151.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f90f43c-2a1a-5c25-8c24-c29c8d08d6ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418420} {:answer/id #uuid "675c188f-e447-5b78-aeb2-a448334d4b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418429} {:answer/id #uuid "a1929e3b-687c-512b-8bea-eac4970832d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418433} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3148ee-e28d-5af1-bd8f-d64f72c5b78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418435} {:answer/id #uuid "c0735613-df61-51c5-a06e-2a93280578c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "675c188f-e447-5b78-aeb2-a448334d4b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418429} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a137f5-8727-53ef-8711-bed34d4307ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad0a7d7a-199c-5de0-b29b-e8636043b58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143398" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{80}$ is a sample of size $n=80$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =-2$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10,$ calculate the value of $\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10$ and the sample size $n=80,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{10}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =0.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59eeeda0-3936-5c54-9132-458d6a1a4733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418427} {:answer/id #uuid "b6aedbee-4603-5029-80ad-06c188d7dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418438} {:answer/id #uuid "c51a71ed-bcc2-559f-8147-183056a30495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418442} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b58351-91b1-5d7d-a4e4-4da284425d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418443} {:answer/id #uuid "ee30fbae-1a7d-5b1f-8690-6db7070cb916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6aedbee-4603-5029-80ad-06c188d7dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418438} :answer-field/id #uuid "1018bdf9-34bc-5ae5-acbb-e8d90df3a9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97d17e06-c282-5844-bc81-dad74b21692f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143402" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two cans is drawn from a population. The population has cans of two different volumes, $0.5\\,\\text{L}$ and $\\text{1}\\,\\text{L},$ distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Calculate $\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the cans in the population have volumes $0.5\\,\\text{L}$ and $1\\,\\text{L},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+3=5$ cans only with the following volumes:\n\n$$\n0.5\\,\\text{L},\\,0.5\\,\\text{L},\\,1\\,\\text{L},\\,1\\,\\text{L},\\,1\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 0.5+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\text{L},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot {0.5}^{2}+3\\cdot {1}^{2})-{0.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3.5)-0.64 \\\\  & =0.7-0.64 \\\\  & =0.06\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample size $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.06}{2}=0.03\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397deda3-79ec-54be-8324-ca298ce871b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418424} {:answer/id #uuid "4d08f4df-3304-5238-9ee4-c8a784de2239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.78$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418425} {:answer/id #uuid "5de5b927-f54f-54d4-bb10-51cc65020f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.86$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418428} {:answer/id #uuid "b226dd71-89e3-53ba-931c-f7309d4db475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.22$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418437} {:answer/id #uuid "bb154b3d-66ec-5d69-b172-03533be8a6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.61$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b226dd71-89e3-53ba-931c-f7309d4db475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.22$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418437} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c53bd6-fdb5-5d5f-980b-becaa30267cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de928809-8125-57ca-bec0-eb6d36dc320c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143428" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four bills is drawn from a population. The population has bills of two different denominations, $$5$ and $$10,$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bills in the population have denominations $$5$ and $$10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bills only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$5,\\,$5,\\,$5,\\,$10\\,$10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(15+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =$7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {5}^{2}+2\\cdot {10}^{2})-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(275)-49 \\\\  & =55-49 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{1.5}≈$1.22.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129fb933-2e4f-545e-993a-52c41611ff34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418419} {:answer/id #uuid "35227a5a-7d12-5671-b51d-fa38508a5e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418421} {:answer/id #uuid "382b8d60-342e-5733-8bbd-fe774dd9f833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418423} {:answer/id #uuid "e28d8097-eae0-5a30-8aa2-4ca2d80389dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418444} {:answer/id #uuid "f93659c0-3601-59d0-b40e-ad89c087e20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35227a5a-7d12-5671-b51d-fa38508a5e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e13886f7-bded-5995-8325-bfe32c2ea9a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aef7c5cc-0e11-515b-bc29-258a1089ae81" :question/math-academy-id "q-143434" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four cellphones is drawn from a population. The population has cellphones of two different screen sizes, $4.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $6\\,\\text{in},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the cellphones in the population have screen sizes of $4.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $6\\,\\text{in},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ cellphones only with the following screen sizes:\n\n$$\n4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,6\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 4.5+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(18+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\text{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {4.5}^{2}+{6}^{2})-{4.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(117)-23.04 \\\\  & =23.4-23.04 \\\\  & =0.36\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.36}{4}=0.09\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.09}=0.3\\,\\text{in}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "353a41d3-498d-5a8a-a9e6-efadecfbdf97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418422} {:answer/id #uuid "51e57186-4049-585f-8889-b1eedaf9cb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418426} {:answer/id #uuid "772d9248-19f9-5fe6-9b77-c1a0f57c290b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62\\,501" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418430} {:answer/id #uuid "8a57d240-911f-55ae-81aa-4c9727b14650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418432} {:answer/id #uuid "bb42c329-8069-528c-b75c-f226cec7f9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a57d240-911f-55ae-81aa-4c9727b14650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418432} :answer-field/id #uuid "795337c1-be28-5aa3-ba9d-a058d2de96cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c52d3d8-d8c0-58d1-b731-d5f1789535ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-143439" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =5$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.02.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{4}=2,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >100 \\\\ n & >{100}^{2} \\\\ n & >10\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=10\\,001.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9b7d3d-e043-540f-ab15-0ac4204800d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418418} {:answer/id #uuid "8a55df69-9335-55f2-8221-d0265b9ad8a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418431} {:answer/id #uuid "a441b3d6-8b1f-55a7-99f5-83230889f2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418434} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7cf6a8-621a-53c3-b1b2-3bc594997a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418436} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b1724-be87-5b72-948d-1118eaf11561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a55df69-9335-55f2-8221-d0265b9ad8a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418431} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afc2bc4-e2a0-5c9c-b2b1-1976022a4510" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "960c78a3-6eed-5896-a952-b3269c984b54" :question/math-academy-id "q-143483" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ people is drawn from a population. The population has people of two different weights, $78\\,\\text{kg}$ and $90\\,\\text{kg},$ distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the people in the population have weights $78\\,\\text{kg}$ and $90\\,\\text{kg},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+1=3$ people only with the following weights:\n\n$$\n78\\,\\text{kg},\\,78\\,\\text{kg},\\,90\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 78+90) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(156+90) \\\\  & =\\frac{246}{3} \\\\  & =82\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot {78}^{2}+{90}^{2})-{82}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(20\\,268)-6\\,724 \\\\  & =6\\,756-6\\,724 \\\\  & =32\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{32},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{n}} & <0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{32}}{0.1} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{{0.1}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3\\,200} \\\\ n & >3\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=3\\,201.$"}]}
